Files from NetBSD
=================

These files are from the NetBSD source tree, branch netbsd-10, commit
a6a6e416dbc433befb1723794fbb058811e3ab64 (2026-10-31), taken from
https://github.com/NetBSD/src. Each keeps its own copyright notice and
licence (BSD-style: the three-clause University of California licence,
the two-clause NetBSD Foundation licence, or public domain for SHA-1).
The paths are NetBSD's.

    sbin/init/                  init(8)
    usr.bin/login/              login(1), without PAM (login.c, common.c)
    usr.bin/su/                 su(1), without PAM (su.c, suutil.c)
    include/ttyent.h            )
    lib/libc/gen/getttyent.c    ) /etc/ttys, for init and login
    lib/libc/stdio/fparseln.c   )
    lib/libcrypt/crypt-sha1.c   )
    lib/libcrypt/hmac.c         ) crypt(3)'s "$sha1$" scheme, for login
    lib/libcrypt/hmac_sha1.c    )
    lib/libcrypt/crypt.h        )
    common/lib/libc/hash/sha1/sha1.c, sys/sys/sha1.h   SHA-1
    lib/libterminfo/             terminfo(3), termcap(3): the terminal
                                descriptions
    usr.bin/tic/, infocmp/, tput/  their compiler, comparer, and tput
    share/terminfo/terminfo      the descriptions (from ncurses; its
                                MIT-style licence, see the file)
    lib/libc/cdb/cdbw.c, common/lib/libc/cdb/cdbr.c, include/cdbw.h,
    sys/sys/cdbr.h, common/lib/libc/stdlib/mi_vector_hash.c
                                the constant database terminfo.cdb is
    sys/sys/bitops.h, sys/sys/queue.h     NetBSD's headers they need
    include/bitstring.h         for nvi
    lib/libutil/efun.c, lib/libc/stdlib/reallocarr.c
    lib/libutil/loginx.c, logoutx.c, logwtmpx.c    utmpx and wtmpx records
    bin/ed/                     ed(1); Belka builds it without DES
    bin/pax/                   pax(1), tar(1), cpio(1), SMALL configuration
    external/bsd/less/dist/     less(1), more(1), using the BSD Less License
    bin/stty/                  stty(1), with System V termios adaptations
    bin/hostname/               hostname(1)
    usr.bin/gzip/              gzip(1), gunzip, gzcat, zcat, over zlib
    usr.bin/sed/                sed(1)
    usr.bin/grep/               grep(1), egrep, fgrep; without NLS catalogues
    external/historical/nawk/dist/   awk(1): the One True AWK, with its
                                parser (ytab.c) as the dist ships it;
                                Lucent's MIT-style licence, see LICENSE
    lib/libc/regex/, include/regex.h   regex(3), with REG_STARTEND and
                                the GNU extensions sed and grep use
    lib/libc/gen/fts.c, include/fts.h  fts(3), for grep -r
    lib/libc/gen/errc.c, verrc.c, warnc.c, vwarnc.c   err(3)'s errc, warnc
    usr.bin/mktemp/, seq/, stat/ (and readlink), find/, xargs/, getconf/
                                the tools ksenon-utils' suite uses; xargs
                                without its pathnames.h, whose licence has
                                an advertising clause (Belka has its own)
    lib/libc/gen/setmode.c      setmode(3) and getmode, for find -perm
    etc/rc.subr, etc/rc.d/      rc.subr, the functions rc.d scripts use, and
                                the scripts DAEMON, LOGIN, NETWORKING,
                                SERVERS, DISKS, CRITLOCALMOUNTED and local;
                                not NetBSD's rc, which needs its own sh,
                                nor rcorder, whose licence has an advertising
                                clause (Belka has its own of each)
    lib/libcurses/              curses(3), over libterminfo; without
                                inchstr.c and instr.c, whose licence has
                                an advertising clause (Belka has its own)
    lib/libc/db/, include/db.h, mpool.h   dbopen(3): btree, hash,
                                recno, mpool
    external/bsd/nvi/dist/      vi(1), ex, view: nvi 1.81.6, its cl,
                                common, ex, vi and regex directories,
                                and ipc and ip, whose headers vi uses;
                                the regex files keep the University of
                                California's advertising clause, which
                                the University withdrew in 1999
    external/bsd/nvi/usr.bin/nvi/   NetBSD's configuration of it

They are built over mlibc with Belka's compat library (usr/lib/compat),
which supplies the BSD interfaces mlibc lacks; see mk/userland.mk.
Belka's changes to these files are kept as separate commits, each
marked in the file.

Not imported: NetBSD's bcrypt and its helpers in libcrypt, and
libcurses's inchstr.c and instr.c, and rcorder, which carry a
four-clause licence with an advertising clause.


Additional utility port details (2026-11-17):
- stty omits unavailable BSD controls, uses System V baud codes, and
  restores speeds through the standard termios functions. Its gfmt1
  parser keeps control-character values hexadecimal.
- less uses the included BSD-style Less License. Belka's definitions are
  in usr/lib/compat/less, using POSIX signals and the termcap API.
- pax builds with SMALL (mtree specification input and alternate user
  databases omitted), using the system passwd/group files. Belka's shims
  are in usr/lib/compat/pax. Compression flags invoke external programs.
